WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.241
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.590
RYAN PENAGOS: The Guardians have
returned for Marvel Studios'

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.880
Guardians of the Galaxy Vol.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:09.680
3.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:10.830
And you know what that means.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:13.390
Our favorite CGI pals
are back together.

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:15.870
That's right, I'm talking
about Rocket and Groot.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:18.720
And I'm here with VFX
supervisor Stephane Ceretti

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.930
to find out how one of the
MCU's most beloved pairs

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.160
is brought to the big screen.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.420
Hi Steph, how are you?

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.457
STEPHANE CERETTI: Hey Ryan,
I'm very good, thank you.

00:00:26.457 --> 00:00:27.690
RYAN PENAGOS: Steph,
you were the VFX

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:30.150
supervisor all the way
back on the first Guardians

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:31.260
of the Galaxy film.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:32.640
What was it like
to get to introduce

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.350
Groot and Rocket to the MCU?

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.540
When we first introduced
them in the first film,

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:38.670
they were such weird
characters that nobody

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.860
was really expecting.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.920
There's that kind of weird
dynamic between the two of them

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.590
that makes them
iconic and you can get

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.855
attached to them quite quickly.

00:00:48.855 --> 00:00:50.250
Now, in this third
Guardians film,

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.480
we dive even deeper
into these characters.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.870
What was it like telling
their stories through VFX?

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:56.820
It's a very emotional
film, this one.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.250
There's all these little
moments that really create

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.390
depth into the character.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.460
They're not just like
animated characters.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:04.110
They're really part of the cast.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.740
So when you begin
work on the film,

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:08.010
how are the actors'
performances captured on set?

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:12.060
We're not using
mocap with the hundreds

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:13.290
of cameras and all that stuff.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:15.150
We do reference shoot.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.620
So we have the actors
there with us there.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:18.870
We're filming the scenes.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:20.940
And we then give it
to the animators.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.670
And it's hand animated,
literally hand animated.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:27.150
So it's a lot of artistry
that comes into this.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:32.610
It's not at all a process
that is automated.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:36.150
So we've really shot the entire
scenes on a virtual set where

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:39.990
we could actually capture the
motion of the camera, which was

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:43.590
very important for James
to use in our full CG

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:47.010
scenes, the same type
of camera operation

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:49.440
that we use throughout the
film to make it seamless

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.000
with the rest of the film.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.190
We have an actor playing Rocket.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.080
We have some
stand-ins for Groot.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.210
And that was really
helpful, actually, for us.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:00.390
We ended up using these
references quite a lot,

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.280
actually, even
sometimes to the point

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.945
where we add little moments
where there's eyes rolling.

00:02:06.945 --> 00:02:08.460
And once you have the
actors' performances,

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.139
what additional visual
reference do you

00:02:10.139 --> 00:02:11.340
capture while you're on set?

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:13.890
We have to shoot what we call
clean plates so that we can

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:15.570
paint out the
actors that are not

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:20.310
supposed to be here and replace
them by all our CGI characters.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.290
So we 3D scan all the sets.

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:25.860
We capture the light with
what we call light probes.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.130
We also add a company called
Legacy that worked with us

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:33.360
and created 3D versions
from the drawings

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.240
from this depth of how
these characters would look

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.200
with perfect fur and
all the mechanical arms

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.140
and things like that so that
we could have a good reference

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.180
on set in terms of lighting.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:50.730
So that what we can do then is
really recreate in the computer

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:53.490
a virtual representation
of the real world

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.800
in terms of lighting,
in terms of the space,

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.720
in terms of
everything that the CG

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.630
actors have to interact with.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:03.180
When we integrate our
characters within the plate,

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:04.800
everything feels
seamless and they

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:08.300
feel like they belong to the
environment they're shot in.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.050
OK, so you've gathered
all that reference.

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:12.900
How do you then actually go
about creating and integrating

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:13.980
the CG characters?

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:15.900
The characters are so
different from humans.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.390
We need to be able to
actually take that performance

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:20.550
from the actor and
really translate

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:24.600
it into the bones and the
skeleton of these animals.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:28.110
We have a lot of shots that are
like close-ups on their eyes.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:29.700
And there's a lot
of emotion going.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:33.360
So we really worked on the
detail of the pupil, the iris,

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.540
everything, the eyes, the way
the eyelids are kind of pushing

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:40.600
opening the color, the level
of darkness around the eyes,

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:42.750
the wetness in the eyes.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:45.390
When you see a CG character
animated, especially

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:48.510
one with fur, there's a lot
of things involved in that

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:49.780
that are extremely complicated.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:51.390
So if there's wind, we
need to have a little bit

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:52.560
of wind in the fur.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.900
If there's two CGI characters
touching each other,

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.080
we need to do, you know,
the fur kind of getting

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.290
displaced by the motion.

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:04.860
For Groot, when he's more young,
his face is more malleable when

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:06.390
you've seen him as Baby Groot.

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:09.120
But as he grows to
become more of a tree,

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.910
we wanted to bring that
kind of level of expression

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:15.480
that was kind of very
detailed in the first film.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:18.480
All these little details
are helping us really bring

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:19.890
the moments where it
needs to be funny,

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:22.350
the moments where we
need to have the emotion.

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:25.800
Once we've shot this, we
start dropping very simple

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.690
models of the characters.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.130
And then we get
into what we call

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:30.630
blocky animation at
the render, which

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:32.100
is a very rough animation.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.530
But it's a little
bit more detailed.

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:38.400
And then as we push through, we
start getting better animation,

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:39.360
more finished animation.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.590
We start getting renders.

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:42.280
It's a very long process.

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:44.850
It's not something you press
a button and you get it.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:49.695
You have to have a very
talented animator and FX team.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:55.120
Everyone is clearly
so freaking talented.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:56.890
Steph, looking back
at all of your work

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:58.180
throughout the
Guardians franchise,

00:04:58.180 --> 00:04:59.800
how does it feel to get
to bring the story of

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:01.180
these characters full circle?

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:03.640
It's been very
gratifying to go

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:07.210
back to the origin of Rocket and
Groot and telling that story.

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:11.200
Because that was a huge focus,
obviously, for me and my teams

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.960
on the first film, to make sure
that these characters would

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:15.820
be able to carry the story.

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:19.210
And now that Rocket is
really the centerpiece

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:22.600
of this third film, it puts
a lot of pressure on us.

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:25.030
But also, it's very
gratifying to see

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:26.395
the result coming together.

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:31.490
Steph, thank you so
much for joining me.

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:32.520
Oh, that was a pleasure.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:34.520
Thank you so much
for inviting me.

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:35.540
Any time.

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:37.070
And everyone out
there, go experience

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:38.960
Rocket and Groot
in Marvel Studios'

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:40.310
Guardians of the Galaxy Vol.

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:42.940
3 in theaters now.

